---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';

const general = [
  {
    q: 'Where do I get the Apple plugin?',
    a: `<p>From the Roblox Creator Store, and only from there — see <a href="/docs/plugin">Install the plugin</a>, which also states plainly whether it is published yet. Roblox splits this into two steps: you acquire the plugin on the web, then install it from inside Studio's Toolbox. There is no one-click install from a web page; Roblox provides no such hand-off.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'There used to be a .rbxm download. Where did it go?',
    a: `<p>Retired. A file you drop into a folder by hand never updates, is not signed by anything, and gets no help from Studio's plugin management window. The Creator Store install is the supported path. Building the plugin yourself is still possible and documented under <a href="/docs/build-from-source">Build the plugin from source</a>, but that is a development path, not an install path.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to know how to script?',
    a: `<p>No, but it helps. Apple writes the Luau for you and explains anything it wrote in plain language (just ask it to explain what it wrote). Creators who can read a little code iterate faster, and plenty of people have learned scripting <em>from</em> Apple's explanations of its own work.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Which Roblox Studio versions are supported?',
    a: `<p>Current production Roblox Studio on Windows and macOS. The plugin uses only stable, documented Studio APIs, so ordinary Studio updates don't break it. If Roblox ships a breaking change, the <a href="/changelog">changelog</a> and the plugin panel will both say so.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can Apple publish my game?',
    a: `<p>No, by design. Apple builds inside your open place; publishing to Roblox stays a human decision made by you, in Studio, with your own account. Apple has no publishing permission over your account and does not ask for one.</p>
      <p>There is one credential you can give it, and only if you want to: an Open Cloud API key, connected under Settings &rarr; Connections, which lets Apple upload images and audio it generates into <em>your</em> Roblox account instead of ours. It is stored encrypted, never shown again, used only for the permissions you ticked, and you can disconnect it at any time. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#roblox-key-refused">What to do if Roblox refuses it</a>.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Apple work with team projects / Team Create?',
    a: `<p>Apple works on the place open in the paired Studio window. In Team Create sessions its edits appear to teammates like any collaborator's edits. One pairing belongs to one Studio window; teammates who want their own Apple chats should use their own accounts and projects.</p>`,
  },
  {
    // "Credits for unexecuted work on our side are refunded" was not true and could not become
    // true: the quota object has no refund route, /spend clamps its amount at zero so a negative
    // cannot be spent, and the only additive route is reachable from the Stripe webhook and an
    // admin key. Nothing is refunded because nothing needs to be — work that never ran reports no
    // compute and is never charged. That is the narrower claim, and it is the one that holds.
    q: 'What happens if I close Studio mid-build?',
    a: `<p>The agent notices it lost its hands and pauses the run with a clear message. Work that never ran is never charged — Credits settle against the compute a call actually reported, and a call that did not happen reports nothing. Reopen the place, reconnect, and ask Apple to continue — the plan and history are still in the project.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Apple made a change I hate. Now what?',
    // "EACH APPLE BATCH IS ONE NATIVE UNDO STEP" WAS THE WRONG UNIT, and it made Ctrl+Z sound like
    // the whole-run exit. The plugin opens one ChangeHistoryService recording per OPERATION
    // (apps/apple-plugin/src/Commands.luau) and the bridge executes a server batch one operation at
    // a time (apps/apple-plugin/src/Bridge.luau), so a batch of six writes is six undo steps. The
    // checkpoint is the single-action exit; Ctrl+Z is the fine-grained one.
    a: `<p>Three exits, in order of size: press <kbd>Ctrl+Z</kbd> in Studio to take back the last change (one undo step per change Apple made, so a multi-step run needs one press per step); restore the automatic checkpoint taken before the run, which undoes the whole run in one action; or just tell Apple what you hate — it can usually fix a wrong turn faster than a rollback.</p>`,
  },
  {
    //[[ THIS ANSWER SAID "Not yet", AND THE FEATURE WAS ALREADY IN THE READER'S OWN ACCOUNT.
    //
    //   The HTTP API shipped the day after this answer was last edited, and nobody came back to it.
    //   `GET /v1` answers 401 with "Provide an API key as `Authorization: Bearer gk_live_…`" on the
    //   live worker — a gated surface, not an absent one — and Settings → Connections → API keys
    //   mints, lists, rotates and revokes keys behind nothing but a signed-in session: no plan gate,
    //   no allowlist. So the docs denied a capability the product was handing out, and the reader
    //   either never found it or found it and stopped believing the manual.
    //
    //   NO LINK TO A DOCS PAGE, BECAUSE THERE IS NO DOCS PAGE. The API describes itself at GET /v1
    //   and GET /v1/openapi.json, both of which need only a valid key, and that is what this points
    //   at. Inventing an /docs/api href here would trade one false sentence for a dead link.
    //
    //   AND NO SDK. The panel in the app names one; `packages/sdk` is `"private": true` and is
    //   published nowhere, so a customer cannot install what that word promises. Naming it here
    //   would spread the same defect onto a second surface.
    q: 'Is there an API or a way to script Apple itself?',
    a: `<p>Yes. There is an HTTP API at <code>/v1</code> on the same host as the app, with scoped keys: chat completions, your projects and their transcripts, starting an agent run, a server-sent event stream of run state, and a Model Context Protocol endpoint for MCP clients.</p>
      <p>Mint a key under <strong>Settings &rarr; Connections &rarr; API keys</strong> in the app — you see the secret once, and you can rotate or revoke it from the same panel. The surface documents itself: <code>GET /v1</code> lists every route and the scope it needs, and <code>GET /v1/openapi.json</code> is the machine-readable description. Both accept any valid key. There is no separate docs page for it yet, and no published client library — the API is the thing that exists.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I report a bug or ask for a feature?',
    a: `<p>Email <a href="mailto:apple.labs.app@gmail.com">apple.labs.app@gmail.com</a>. Include the project name and roughly when it happened; screenshots welcome. Feedback lands directly with the people who build the product — there is no ticket abyss.</p>`,
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="FAQ — Apple Docs"
  description="Quick answers about getting the plugin, scripting knowledge, Studio versions, publishing, Team Create, interrupted builds, undo and rollback, and reporting bugs."
  heading="Frequently asked questions"
>
  <p>
    Answers to the questions that reach our inbox most. Pricing and quota questions live on the
    <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>; data questions on
    <a href="/docs/privacy-and-data">privacy &amp; data</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="faq-block">
    <FAQ items={general} />
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .faq-block {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
</style>
